{
  "dom_id": "step-q71776",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Solve the equation $2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}+24x=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2x.$ We can factor out the $2x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}+24x & =0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}+7x+12) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${x}^{2}+7x+12$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x({x}^{2}+7x+12) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+4)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=-4,$ and $x=-3.$",
